+
+## Amendment 7 -- risk-weighted sector neutrality, MENTOR-PROMPTED and POST-HOC (2026-10-28)
+
+The mentor review showed that dollar-neutral is not sector-neutral: Amendment
+5's book holds equal long and short DOLLARS in every GICS sector, but when a
+sector's longs move more with the sector than its shorts (or less), the sector
+still carries net exposure. This amendment changes one thing, the sector step
+of `group_balance`: each sector is balanced on dollars times each name's beta
+to its own sector, sum(long $ x beta) = sum(short $ x beta), still by scaling
+the heavier side down. Sector beta is a Dimson beta (lag 0 + lag 1) on the 252
+trailing trading days up to the day before formation (no look-ahead), against
+the equal-weighted daily return of that sector's candidates, clipped to
+[0.3, 3.0], and set to 1.0 for a name with fewer than 126 usable days. The
+251 trading days before each window are loaded for this estimate only, so the
+first formations of a window get real betas. The industry cap, name caps and
+gross stay in plain dollars, as in Amendment 5. Chosen after mentor review
+(Oct 28) and BEFORE any run with it. This overrides Amendment 5's statement
+that it was the last construction change before the presentation.
+
+- `--sector-neutral --sector-beta` (label suffix `_sbeta`); formation logs add
+  `max_abs_sector_net_risk`, the largest |sector net| in beta-weighted dollars.
+- Reported next to Amendment 5, with and without the futures hedge (`f11`),
+  whatever it shows. The headline (`test_M`) is unchanged.
+- Amendment 6 is reserved for the turnover rows.
